        "answerKey": "1. Statement: If f(x,y) is a homogeneous function of degree k, then x(df/dx) + y(df/dy) = k f(x,y).\n2. Proof:\n   Start with the homogeneity definition: f(tx, ty) = t^k f(x,y).\n3. Differentiate both sides with respect to t using the Chain Rule.\n   Left Side: Let u = tx, v = ty. Differentiating f(u, v) w.r.t t gives:\n   (df/du)(du/dt) + (df/dv)(dv/dt) = (df/du)(x) + (df/dv)(y).\n   Right Side: Differentiating t^k f(x,y) w.r.t t gives: k t^(k-1) f(x,y).\n4. Equate both sides:\n   x (df/du) + y (df/dv) = k t^(k-1) f(x,y).\n5. Substitute t = 1. This gives u = x and v = y, resulting in:\n   x (df/dx) + y (df/dy) = k f(x,y).\nThis completes the proof."
