        "answerKey": "1. Statement: Any vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ (vanishing at infinity) can be written as $\\mathbf{F} = \\mathbf{F}_l + \\mathbf{F}_t$ where $\\mathbf{F}_l = -\\nabla\\phi$ (longitudinal, irrotational: $\\nabla\\times\\mathbf{F}_l = 0$) and $\\mathbf{F}_t = \\nabla\\times\\mathbf{A}$ (transverse, solenoidal: $\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf{F}_t = 0$).\n2. The scalar potential: $\\phi(\\mathbf{r}) = \\frac{1}{4\\pi}\\int\\frac{\\nabla'\\cdot\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}')}{|\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{r}'|}d^3r'$.\n3. The vector potential: $\\mathbf{A}(\\mathbf{r}) = \\frac{1}{4\\pi}\\int\\frac{\\nabla'\\times\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}')}{|\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{r}'|}d^3r'$.\n4. Proof uses the identity $\\mathbf{F} = -\\nabla(\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf{G}) + \\nabla\\times(\\nabla\\times\\mathbf{G})$ where $\\mathbf{G}$ solves $\\nabla^2\\mathbf{G} = -\\mathbf{F}$ (vector Poisson equation)."
