        "question": "Define a mixed tensor of type $(1,1)$ (rank 2). Give the coordinate transformation law. Show that the Kronecker delta $\\delta^i_j$ is a mixed tensor of type $(1,1)$.",
        "answerKey": "1. A mixed tensor of type $(1,1)$: quantities $T^i_j$ (one upper, one lower index) that transform as $T'^i_k = \\frac{\\partial x'^i}{\\partial x^l}\\frac{\\partial x^m}{\\partial x'^k} T^l_m$.\n2. Proof for $\\delta^i_j$: Under transformation, $\\delta'^i_k = \\frac{\\partial x'^i}{\\partial x^l}\\frac{\\partial x^m}{\\partial x'^k}\\delta^l_m = \\frac{\\partial x'^i}{\\partial x^l}\\frac{\\partial x^l}{\\partial x'^k}$.\n3. By the chain rule, $\\frac{\\partial x'^i}{\\partial x^l}\\frac{\\partial x^l}{\\partial x'^k} = \\frac{\\partial x'^i}{\\partial x'^k} = \\delta^i_k$.\n4. So $\\delta'^i_k = \\delta^i_k$: the Kronecker delta has the same components in all coordinate systems, confirming it is a tensor (an invariant/isotropic tensor).\n5. The Kronecker delta represents the identity transformation in any basis."
